  "worked_solution": "Finding the kernel of $\\,T\\,$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $\\,T.$\n\nFor the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSetting $x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y.$ From the second equation, we obtain $y=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we have $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $T$ is $\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}.$",
